// lst -- writing through a list you do not own.
//
// A list passed as a *parameter* is an lvalue; an imported global is not. The
// compiler only recognises an index-assignment whose target starts with a plain
// identifier, so `(import xs)[i] = v;` is not an assignment at all -- it is a
// comparison whose result is thrown away. It used to compile cleanly and do
// nothing, which made it the language's most expensive trap; it is now rejected,
// and the diagnostic names this function as the fix:
//
//     error: an imported list cannot be index-assigned directly; pass it to a
//     helper that takes the list as a parameter (lset(xs, i, v))
//
// So every write to an exported list goes through lset, defined once for the
// whole program. That is the one place where idstd deliberately takes a bare,
// unprefixed name: the compiler's own diagnostic already spells it, hundreds of
// existing `id` programs already define it, and a prefixed `lst_set` would leave
// every one of them with a duplicate-logic error and no way out.
//
// Lifted from idem/engine/core/util/lst/lst.id.

lset(int[] xs, int i, int v) {
  xs[i] = v;
} return void;
([7, 8], 0, 5):([5, 8], 0, 5)
([7, 8], 1, 5):([7, 5], 1, 5)

lget(int[] xs, int i) {
  int ret_i = xs[i];
} return int ret_i;
([4, 9], 0):(4)
([4, 9], 1):(9)

sset(string[] strs, int i, string s) {
  strs[i] = s;
} return void;
(["a", "b"], 0, "z"):(["z", "b"], 0, "z")
(["a", "b"], 1, "z"):(["a", "z"], 1, "z")
